basicly a taskbar, or just add the systems taskbar to the operator monitor.

---
WO-308 (split operator monitor from pointer confine) — DONE, commit follows.
  New setting, Device View -> GPU port inspector, next to "Operator monitor":
    Confine: auto (default, byte-identical to before) | on | off.
  'off' still makes the port the operator monitor for GUI/helper placement, it just never
  cages the mouse to it. Verified live on this box: switching to 'off' stopped the running
  barrier process immediately (journal: "SKIP - pointerConfine_off"), switching back to
  'auto' brought it back ("RUN - operator_monitor_port_3"). Box left in its normal 'auto'
  state afterward - nothing changed for you unless you go flip it.
  Every decision now logs one line (RUN/SKIP + why), same style as the WO-290 monitor
  picker. NEEDS KIOSK RELOAD for the new dropdown to appear.

---
WO-309 (async device-view probes) — DONE, commit follows.
  GET /api/device-view no longer blocks the whole server while it reads xrandr.
  Verified live: a slow (366ms) device-view fetch ran while I fired 6 AMCP round-trips
  at the same time - all 6 stayed at 0-1ms, same as with nothing else happening.
  Scoped narrower than "convert everything": only the actual GET /api/device-view path
  was converted. ~20 other places that read xrandr (bootstrap scripts, layout math used
  deep inside config generation) were left exactly as they are - they were never slow
  enough to matter and touching them risked a much bigger, riskier refactor for no
  measured benefit. Full reasoning is in the WO-309 file if you want it.
  Nothing to test in the UI for this one - it's a background responsiveness fix, not a
  visible feature. No kiosk reload needed (server-side only).

---
"operator gui refuses to run 2160p50" + "native res not in dropdown" — DONE, commit follows.
  First question (why 2160p50 doesn't run): Caspar itself is 100% correct - config, and
  its own log across 3 restarts, all confirm 3840x2160 window created fine. The real gap
  was one level down: the operator monitor's ACTUAL xrandr output was still 1920x1080 -
  the Caspar video-mode field and the physical GPU port's OS resolution are two SEPARATE
  settings, and only the first one had been changed. That part needed no code fix, just
  pointing at the right control ("Custom OS resolution" in the GPU port inspector).
  Second question (the real bug): confirmed. The inspector's "Native mode" row correctly
  reads 3840x2160@50 from the monitor's own EDID, but the resolution DROPDOWN only lists
  modes xrandr already knows about - which for this port was 1920x1080 and 1280x1024,
  nothing at 4K. "Custom (register RandR mode)" is the correct way to add a mode xrandr
  doesn't have yet, but it defaulted to a generic 1920x1080@50 guess with zero awareness
  of the EDID value shown two rows up - so you had to hand-retype numbers the app already
  knew were right.
  Fixed: opening that panel now defaults straight to Custom, ALREADY filled in with
  3840x2160 @ 50 - matching "Native mode" exactly, nothing to type. The Custom option's
  label also now says "EDID native 3840x2160 @ 50" so it's clear that's the correct
  choice, not a workaround.
  NEEDS KIOSK RELOAD. Client-only, no service restart needed.


